 the metrics aren\'t coming up. Alternatively, if the pod is running but the container is exiting, that\'s another issue.\n\nAnother possibility is that the metrics endpoint for user-service isn\'t accessible, so even though the pod is running, the up{} metric isn\'t being reported. Checking the pod\'s logs would help determine if there\'s an error in the application startup or if the metrics server isn\'t configured correctly.\n\nSo, the correct tool here is exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command "kubectl get pods -n social-network" to list all pods and find the user-service pod\'s name. Then, use that name to get the logs. But since the user can\'t specify the pod name directly, maybe the logs command needs the pod name. However, the previous attempt failed because the pod name wasn\'t provided. Therefore, first, retrieve the pod names to get the correct one.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \n"kubectl get pods -n social-network"  \nTo identify the exact pod name for user-service, I need to list all pods in the social-network namespace. This will allow me to determine the correct pod name for logging and further inspection, as the previous attempt failed due to an incomplete pod identifier.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 501, 'prompt_tokens': 2677, 'total_tokens': 3178, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc5d-74d2-7370-a3cd-9d43a795f1ff-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2677, 'output_tokens': 501, 'total_tokens': 3178}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='8c61d50c-f2ed-4491-bb76-aa987658e77f'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose an issue with the user-service in their social network application. They\'ve already checked the deployments and saw that user-service is running with READY=1/1, but the metrics query for up{job="user-service"} returned an empty result. That means the user-service isn\'t actually up, even though the deployment says it is.\n\nSo, the next step is to check the actual pods. The user tried to get the logs but didn\'t have the correct pod name. They need to list all the pods in the social-network namespace to find the exact name of the user-service pod. Once they have the pod name, they can check the logs for any errors or issues that might be causing the service to not be up. \n\nThe command "kubectl get pods -n social-network" will list all the pods, and they can look for the one related to user-service. After getting the pod name, they can use "kubectl logs" with the specific pod name to view the logs. If the logs show errors, that could indicate the root cause, like a configuration mistake, missing dependencies, or a crash loop. \n\nAlternatively, they might also check the pod\'s status with "kubectl describe pod <pod-name> -n social-network" to see if there are any events or reasons why the pod isn\'t running as expected. But first, getting the list of pods is essential to proceed further.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pods -n social-network"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-97bd22d8f0af68b5', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 328, 'prompt_tokens': 3649, 'total_tokens': 3977, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc5d-8bdd-7cb1-af1f-77ff955b79e2-0', tool_calls=[{'name