he metrics still don\'t show any data. The next step is to check the logs of the user-service pod to see if there are any errors or issues causing it to not report metrics. \n\nThe \'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd\' tool can be used to retrieve the logs. The correct command would be \'kubectl logs user-service-559b79c875-2x7rc -n social-network\'. This will provide insights into any runtime errors, configuration issues, or problems with the metrics server. If the logs show that the metrics endpoint is not configured correctly or the service is crashing, that would explain the missing metrics. Alternatively, if there\'s a network issue preventing the metrics from being scraped, that\'s another angle to explore. Checking the logs is crucial to pinpoint the exact cause of the problem.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \n"kubectl logs user-service-559b79c875-2x7rc -n social-network"  \nThe user-service pod is listed as "Running," but metrics show no uptime. To investigate why the service isn\'t reporting metrics, I need to inspect its logs for runtime errors, configuration issues, or problems with the metrics endpoint. This will help identify if the service is crashing, misconfigured, or failing to expose metrics.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 435, 'prompt_tokens': 3864, 'total_tokens': 4299, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc5d-9e0e-7201-8bbe-8dfedf085114-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3864, 'output_tokens': 435, 'total_tokens': 4299}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='2d766a7f-bacb-4279-8d43-a3cb7f71e0f0'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose why the user-service in their social network application isn\'t reporting metrics, even though the pod is marked as "Running". They\'ve already checked the deployments and pods, and the user-service pod is up. But when they tried to get metrics for the user-service, the result was empty, which is odd.\n\nSo, the next step is to look into the logs of the user-service pod. The previous attempt to get logs failed because the pod name wasn\'t fully specified. Now, they\'ve identified the correct pod name: user-service-559b79c875-2x7rc. The user is using the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd tool to fetch the logs. \n\nBy checking the logs, they can look for any errors or exceptions that might indicate why the service isn\'t functioning as expected. Common issues could be misconfigured environment variables, failed connections to other services, or problems with the metrics server. The logs should provide clues about the service\'s runtime behavior. If there are errors in the logs, that could point to the root cause. If not, they might need to check other aspects like resource limits or network policies. But starting with the logs is the logical next step.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl logs user-service-559b79c875-2x7rc -n social-network"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9434f853f5346697', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 308, 'prompt_tokens': 4868, 'total_tokens': 5176, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc5d-b29c-71e1-b2c7-2b930d4ab789-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl logs user-service-559b79c875-2x7rc -n social-network'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9434f853f5346697', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4868, 'output_tokens': 308, 'total_tokens': 5176}), ToolMessage(content='[2026-Mar-17 15:12:37.351446] <info>: (UserHandler.h:932:GetMachineId) MAC address = 6e:e4:65:f0:12:7d\n[2026-Mar-17 15:12:37.351755] <info>: (UserService.cpp:63:main) machine_id = 413\n[2026-Mar-17 15:12:37.597658] <info>: (UserService.cpp:94:main) Starting the user-service server ...\n', id='7a44a769-ed0b-412e-83a5-a9c78f4e9228', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-9434f853f5346697'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='ebe0ecf2-d1a7-4ea6-9cdd-0e8f53d880b7'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user-service pod is running, but the metrics show it\'s not up. The logs indicate the service started, but maybe the metrics endpoint isn\'t working. I should check the metrics for the user-service to see if there\'s an issue with the exporter or the configuration. Using get_metrics with the correct query would help. The previous attempt used "up{job=\\"user-service\\"}" but got an empty result. Maybe the job name is different or the metrics aren\'t being scraped properly. I need to verify the metrics configuration and ensure the exporter is running. Alternatively, checking the service\'s dependencies via the dependency graph might show if there\'s a problem with the metrics service itself.\n</think>\n\nget_metrics  \n"up{job=\\"user-service-metrics\\"}"  \nThe user-service logs indicate the server started, but metrics show no uptime. This suggests the metrics exporter might be misconfigured or not scraping data. Querying with a more specific job name ("user-service-metrics") will confirm if the metrics endpoint is operational or if there\'s a scraping failure.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 222, 'prompt_tokens': 4220, 'total_tokens': 4442, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc5d-c444-7bc0-aaed-740ea48b9473-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4220, 'output_tokens': 222, 'total_tokens': 4442}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='2c05ffbc-2269-4b42-a611-f9aa84d6a066'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose why the user-service in their social network application isn\'t reporting metrics, even though the pod is running. They checked the logs and saw the service started, but the metrics query returned empty.\n\nFirst, they used get_metrics with "up{job=\\"user-service\\"}" and got an empty result. Then they tried a more specific job name, "user-service-metrics". The next step is to check if the metrics endpoint is actually serving data. \n\nMaybe the Prometheus query is incorrect. The user should verify the exact job name used by the metrics exporter. If the exporter isn\'t configured properly, Prometheus won\'t scrape the metrics. They could check the service\'s configuration to see if the metrics endpoint is exposed and accessible. \n\nAlternatively, they might need to look into the metrics endpoint directly. Using a tool like curl to check the endpoint\'s response could help. But since the available tools are limited, the next best step is to use get_metrics again with the correct job name. If that still returns nothing, it might indicate a problem with the metrics setup, like the exporter not running or the port not being exposed. \n\nThey should also check the deployment and service YAMLs to ensure the metrics port is correctly exposed and that the Prometheus service is configured to scrape that endpoint. But given the tools available, the next action is to query the metrics again with the specific job name to confirm if data is being collected.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"query": "up{job=\\"user-service-metrics\\"}"}', 'name': 'get_metrics'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-92ea2255fef9703a', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 329, 'prompt_tokens': 5199, 'total_tokens': 5528, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc5d-cec5-7942-8753-1f99abbbdcca-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'get_metrics', 'args': {'query': 'up{job="user-service-metrics"}'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-92ea2255fef9703a', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 5199, 'output_tokens': 329, 'total_tokens': 5528}), ToolMessage(content='("{\'resultType\': \'vector\', \'result\': []}", 12)', id='220cc09a-aaf5-4ca6-a154-d1a075037f28', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-92ea2255fef9703a'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='76633400-a2de-420d-b90d-cb332122214b'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user-service pod is running, and the logs show the server started, but the metrics aren\'t showing up. The initial check with `up{job="user-service"}` didn\'t return anything, and when I tried a more specific job name `user-service-metrics`, it still came back empty. \n\nHmm, maybe the metrics endpoint isn\'t exposed correctly. The service might not be publishing metrics on the expected port or path. I should check the deployment configuration to see if the metrics endpoint is set up properly. Also, maybe the service is using a different port for metrics than what\'s configured in Prometheus.\n\nWait, the user-service is a microservice that communicates via Thrift RPCs. Could there be a network issue preventing Prometheus from scraping the metrics? Or maybe the metrics exporter isn\'t running inside the container. Let me check the container\'s ports and if the metrics server is listening.\n\nI should use `kubectl describe pod` to look at the container\'s ports and see if the metrics port is exposed. Also, checking the service YAML to see if there\'s a service exposing the metrics endpoint. If the service isn\'t set up, Prometheus won\'t be able to scrape the metrics, leading to no data being collected.\n\nAnother angle: maybe the metrics job name in Prometheus is incorrect. The logs mention the service started, but if the metrics endpoint is on a different path or port, the scrape configuration in Prometheus might not be matching. I need to verify the scrape targets in Prometheus to ensure they\'re correctly configured for the user-service\'s metrics.\n\nAlternatively, the container might not have the necessary environment variables or configurations to expose the metrics. Checking the deployment\'s environment variables and any ConfigMaps or Secrets that might be relevant could help. If the metrics exporter is missing or misconfigured, the service won\'t send data to Prometheus.\n\nI should also check if there are any events related to the pod that might indicate issues with the container starting or network problems. Using `kubectl describe pod` would show any events or errors that occurred during the pod\'s lifecycle. If there\'s a crash loop or failed startup, that could explain why metrics aren\'t being collected even though the pod is marked as running.\n\nPutting it all together, the next step is to describe the pod to check for any issues in the container\'s configuration, ports, and events. That should help identify if the metrics endpoint is properly set up and if there are any underlying issues preventing the metrics from being scraped.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \n"kubectl describe pod user-service-559b79c875-2x7rc -n social-network"  \nTo investigate why the user-service metrics are not being scraped, I need to examine the pod\'s detailed configuration, including container ports, environment variables, and events. This will reveal if the metrics endpoint is exposed correctly, if the container is misconfigured, or if there are runtime errors preventing metric exposure.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': Usage(completion_tokens=603, prompt_tokens=4399, total_tokens=5002, completion_tokens_details=None, prompt_tokens_details=None), 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc5d-df99-7893-b4be-cb9dedf38642-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4399, 'output_tokens': 603, 'total_tokens': 5002}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='08383615-acdb-4fdb-9bcf-03dcbf63b81f')]]
