ht be misconfigured or not scraping data. Querying with a more specific job name ("user-service-metrics") will confirm if the metrics endpoint is operational or if there\'s a scraping failure.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 222, 'prompt_tokens': 4220, 'total_tokens': 4442, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc5d-c444-7bc0-aaed-740ea48b9473-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4220, 'output_tokens': 222, 'total_tokens': 4442}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='2c05ffbc-2269-4b42-a611-f9aa84d6a066'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose why the user-service in their social network application isn\'t reporting metrics, even though the pod is running. They checked the logs and saw the service started, but the metrics query returned empty.\n\nFirst, they used get_metrics with "up{job=\\"user-service\\"}" and got an empty result. Then they tried a more specific job name, "user-service-metrics". The next step is to check if the metrics endpoint is actually serving data. \n\nMaybe the Prometheus query is incorrect. The user should verify the exact job name used by the metrics exporter. If the exporter isn\'t configured properly, Prometheus won\'t scrape the metrics. They could check the service\'s configuration to see if the metrics endpoint is exposed and accessible. \n\nAlternatively, they might need to look into the metrics endpoint directly. Using a tool like curl to check the endpoint\'s response could help. But since the available tools are limited, the next best step is to use get_metrics again with the correct job name. If that still returns nothing, it might indicate a problem with the metrics setup, like the exporter not running or the port not being exposed. \n\nThey should also check the deployment and service YAMLs to ensure the metrics port is correctly exposed and that the Prometheus service is configured to scrape that endpoint. But given the tools available, the next action is to query the metrics again with the specific job name to confirm if data is being collected.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"query": "up{job=\\"user-service-metrics\\"}"}', 'name': 'get_metrics'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-92ea2255fef9703a', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 329, 'prompt_tokens': 5199, 'total_tokens': 5528, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc5d-cec5-7942-8753-1f99abbbdcca-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'get_metrics', 'args': {'query': 'up{job="user-service-metrics"}'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-92ea2255fef9703a', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 5199, 'output_tokens': 329, 'total_tokens': 5528}), ToolMessage(content='("{\'resultType\': \'vector\', \'result\': []}", 12)', id='220cc09a-aaf5-4ca6-a154-d1a075037f28', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-92ea2255fef9703a'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='76633400-a2de-420d-b90d-cb332122214b'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user-service pod is running, and the logs show the server started, but the metrics aren\'t showing up. The initial check with `up{job="user-service"}` didn\'t return anything, and when I tried a more specific job name `user-service-metrics`, it still came back empty. \n\nHmm, maybe the metrics endpoint isn\'t exposed correctly. The service might not be publishing metrics on the expected port or path. I should check the deployment configuration to see if the metrics endpoint is set up properly. Also, maybe the service is using a different port for metrics than what\'s configured in Prometheus.\n\nWait, the user-service is a microservice that communicates via Thrift RPCs. Could there be a network issue preventing Prometheus from scraping the metrics? Or maybe the metrics exporter isn\'t running inside the container. Let me check the container\'s ports and if the metrics server is listening.\n\nI should use `kubectl describe pod` to look at the container\'s ports and see if the metrics port is exposed. Also, checking the service YAML to see if there\'s a service exposing the metrics endpoint. If the service isn\'t set up, Prometheus won\'t be able to scrape the metrics, leading to no data being collected.\n\nAnother angle: maybe the metrics job name in Prometheus is incorrect. The logs mention the service started, but if the metrics endpoint is on a different path or port, the scrape configuration in Prometheus might not be matching. I need to verify the scrape targets in Prometheus to ensure they\'re correctly configured for the user-service\'s metrics.\n\nAlternatively, the container might not have the necessary environment variables or configurations to expose the metrics. Checking the deployment\'s environment variables and any ConfigMaps or Secrets that might be relevant could help. If the metrics exporter is missing or misconfigured, the service won\'t send data to Prometheus.\n\nI should also check if there are any events related to the pod that might indicate issues with the container starting or network problems. Using `kubectl describe pod` would show any events or errors that occurred during the pod\'s lifecycle. If there\'s a crash loop or failed startup, that could explain why metrics aren\'t being collected even though the pod is marked as running.\n\nPutting it all together, the next step is to describe the pod to check for any issues in the container\'s configuration, ports, and events. That should help identify if the metrics endpoint is properly set up and if there are any underlying issues preventing the metrics from being scraped.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \n"kubectl describe pod user-service-559b79c875-2x7rc -n social-network"  \nTo investigate why the user-service metrics are not being scraped, I need to examine the pod\'s detailed configuration, including container ports, environment variables, and events. This will reveal if the metrics endpoint is exposed correctly, if the container is misconfigured, or if there are runtime errors preventing metric exposure.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 603, 'prompt_tokens': 4399, 'total_tokens': 5002, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc5d-df99-7893-b4be-cb9dedf38642-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4399, 'output_tokens': 603, 'total_tokens': 5002}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='08383615-acdb-4fdb-9bcf-03dcbf63b81f'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user-service pod is listed as Running, but the metrics aren't showing up. The logs show the service started, but the metrics query returns empty. So, the next step is to check the pod's details.\n\nI called kubectl describe pod to get more info. The response will include things like container statuses, ports, environment variables, and events. If the metrics endpoint isn't exposed correctly, maybe the container isn't listening on the right port. Or perhaps the service account doesn't have the right permissions to scrape metrics. Also, checking the events can show if there were any failures during deployment or runtime. \n\nLooking at the describe output, if the container's ports aren't set up properly, that's a problem. Or if the environment variables for the metrics exporter are missing, like the port or the endpoint. Also, if there are any errors in the pod's status, that could indicate issues with the container's health checks or resource limits. \n\nOnce I have that info, I can determine if the metrics endpoint is configured correctly and if there's a problem with the scraping setup. If the port isn't exposed, I might need to adjust the service or deployment configuration. If the exporter isn't running, checking the logs again or the container's entrypoint could help. \n\nSo, the next tool call should be to describe the pod to get all these details and identify the root cause.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl describe pod user-service-559b79c875-2x7rc -n social-network"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-927dd23867c7ea12', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 341, 'prompt_tokens': 5399, 'total_tokens': 5740, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc5d-fbcb-7093-86e0-3cb934065877-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl describe pod user-service-559b79c875-2x7rc -n social-network'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-927dd23867c7ea12', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 5399, 'output_tokens': 341, 'total_tokens': 5740}), 