            node.kubernetes.io/not-ready:NoExecute op=Exists for 300s\n                             node.kubernetes.io/unreachable:NoExecute op=Exists for 300s\nEvents:\n  Type    Reason     Age    From               Message\n  ----    ------     ----   ----               -------\n  Normal  Scheduled  5m11s  default-scheduler  Successfully assigned social-network/user-service-559b79c875-2x7rc to node2.htliang2-agent.insai-pg0.apt.emulab.net\n  Normal  Pulling    4m54s  kubelet            spec.containers{user-service}: Pulling image "docker.io/deathstarbench/social-network-microservices:latest"\n  Normal  Pulled     3m13s  kubelet            spec.containers{user-service}: Successfully pulled image "docker.io/deathstarbench/social-network-microservices:latest" in 2.848s (1m40.245s including waiting). Image size: 94999339 bytes.\n  Normal  Created    3m12s  kubelet            spec.containers{user-service}: Created container: user-service\n  Normal  Started    3m11s  kubelet            spec.containers{user-service}: Started container user-service\n', id='d736be5f-8bdf-45f5-bbac-64d424ccf90c', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-927dd23867c7ea12'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='87302b38-3a58-4912-b01b-bc7e50c6a085'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user-service pod is running, but the metrics aren\'t showing up. The logs show the service started, but when I checked the metrics with the job name "user-service-metrics", there were no results. The pod\'s describe output shows the container is running and the image was pulled successfully. The environment variables are empty, but there\'s a ConfigMap mounted. Maybe the metrics endpoint isn\'t exposed correctly. The container\'s port is 9090/TCP, but maybe the service isn\'t set up to expose that port. Wait, the user-service might be using a different port for metrics. Or perhaps the Prometheus isn\'t scraping the correct endpoint. Let me check the service configuration for user-service. If there\'s a service object for user-service, it should map the container port to a cluster IP. If the service isn\'t created, or if the target port is wrong, the metrics won\'t be scraped. So I should check the services in the namespace to see if user-service has a corresponding service. If not, that\'s the issue. Alternatively, maybe the metrics are on a different path or port. Let me use the get_services tool to see if the user-service is listed, and then check the service details.\n</think>\n\nget_services  \nTo confirm if the user-service has a corresponding Kubernetes Service object, I need to check the list of services in the social-network namespace. If the service is missing or misconfigured, Prometheus won\'t be able to scrape metrics from the user-service pod. This will help identify if the service exposure is the root cause of the missing metrics.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 331, 'prompt_tokens': 5585, 'total_tokens': 5916, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc5e-0f5c-73d0-8d7b-bd11e06a45f1-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 5585, 'output_tokens': 331, 'total_tokens': 5916}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='c612a4d6-27e6-4f1a-b468-9c2173a02a7f'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose why the user-service in their social network application isn\'t reporting metrics. They\'ve already checked the pods and deployments, and the user-service pod is running. The logs show the service started, but the metrics query returns nothing. \n\nThe next step was to check the pod\'s description, which showed the container is running and the ports are set. The image used is from a specific registry, and there\'s no environment variables listed. The metrics job name they tried was "user-service-metrics," which didn\'t return any results.\n\nNow, the user is suggesting using the get_services tool to check if the user-service has a corresponding Kubernetes Service. That makes sense because if the service isn\'t properly defined, Prometheus might not be able to scrape the metrics. The service would need to expose the metrics endpoint on the correct port. \n\nSo, by retrieving the list of services in the social-network namespace, they can verify if there\'s a service named something like user-service-metrics or if the existing service is misconfigured. If the service is missing or the port is incorrect, that would explain why the metrics aren\'t being collected. \n\nTherefore, calling get_services will help confirm the presence and configuration of the service associated with user-service, which is crucial for metric scraping.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{}', 'name': 'get_services'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-803a5796973bff08', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 286, 'prompt_tokens': 6560, 'total_tokens': 6846, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc5e-1f6c-7751-aa91-7a3080f55116-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'get_services', 'args': {}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-803a5796973bff08', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 6560, 'output_tokens': 286, 'total_tokens': 6846}), ToolMessage(content='meta=None content=[TextContent(type=\'text\', text="[\'recommendation\', \'quote\', \'cart\', \'product-catalog\', \'fraud-detection\', \'load-generator\', \'flagd\', \'payment\', \'image-provider\', \'ad\', \'frontend\', \'shipping\', \'jaeger-all-in-one\', \'frontend-proxy\', \'checkout\', \'accounting\', \'product-reviews\', \'currency\', \'email\']", annotations=None)] isError=False', id='ee92cb7c-3d78-4242-98c1-29118b010a21', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-803a5796973bff08'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='0472ef9e-2196-4b39-836c-df859bd99855'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose why the user-service in the social-network application isn\'t reporting metrics. So far, they checked the pods, deployments, logs, and the service list. The metrics query for "up{job=\\"user-service-metrics\\"}" returned empty, which suggests that the metrics endpoint isn\'t being scraped.\n\nFirst, they used get_services to list all services in the namespace. The response didn\'t include user-service, which might mean that the service isn\'t properly defined. But wait, the user-service pod is running, so there should be a corresponding service to expose its metrics. \n\nWait, maybe the service is named differently. The user-service might be using a different job name for metrics, like "user-service" instead of "user-service-metrics". The previous get_metrics query used "user-service-metrics", which might not exist. \n\nAlternatively, the service might not be created at all. The get_services tool\'s output didn\'t list user-service, so maybe the service is missing. But the deployment exists, so the service should be there. Or maybe the service is misconfigured, not exposing the correct port or path for metrics.\n\nAnother angle: the logs show the user-service started, but maybe the metrics endpoint isn\'t running. The container might not have the metrics exporter (like Prometheus) configured. The logs don\'t mention any errors related to metrics, so maybe the issue is with the service configuration.\n\nThe next step is to check if there\'s a Kubernetes Service for user-service. Since get_services didn\'t return it, the user should check the services in the namespace. They can use kubectl get services -n social-network to confirm. If the service is missing, that\'s the root cause. If it\'s present but misconfigured, then the metrics endpoint isn\'t accessible.\n\nWait, the user already tried get_services, which returned a list that didn\'t include user-service. So the service might not exist. But the deployment is there. Maybe the service was never created, or it\'s named something else. Alternatively, the service might be in a different namespace, but the user specified the social-network namespace.\n\nSo the next tool should be to check the services in the social-network namespace using kubectl get services -n social-network. If the user-service service isn\'t there, that\'s the problem. If it is, check the port and targetPort to ensure metrics are exposed correctly. Also, verify that the service is correctly linked to the pod\'s port (like 9090/TCP from the pod description).\n\nAlternatively, the user could check the service configuration using kubectl describe service user-service -n social-network if it exists. But since get_services didn\'t list it, the first step is to confirm its existence.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \n"kubectl get services -n social-network"  \nTo verify if the user-service has a corresponding Kubernetes Service object exposing its metrics endpoint, I need to check the list of services in the social-network namespace. If the service is missing or misconfigured (e.g., incorrect port mapping, no exposure), Prometheus won\'t scrape metrics, leading to the empty result from the metrics query. This will confirm whether service exposure is the root cause.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 664, 'prompt_tokens': 5822, 'total_tokens': 6486, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc5e-2ea5-7221-9fff-7b1efd10ca37-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 5822, 'output_tokens': 664, 'total_tokens': 6486}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='5a047e99-92c9-4a82-825a-00ad1fb15a4c'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose why the user-service in their social network application isn't reporting metrics, even though the pod is running. They've already checked the pods, deployments, and logs, but the metrics query returns nothing.\n\nFirst, they used get_services from Grafana, which listed services like recommendation, quote, cart, etc., but not user-service. That's a problem because if there's no service for user-service, Prometheus can't scrape its metrics. But wait, maybe the service is named differently? Like maybe it's not explicitly listed because it's managed by another tool or has a different name.\n\nNext step is to check the actual Kubernetes services in the social-network namespace. By running kubectl get services -n social-network, they can see if there's a service associated with user-service. If the service isn't there, that's the root cause. If it is, maybe the port or target is misconfigured. The logs showed the user-service is running on port 9090, so the service should expose that port. If the service is missing, they need to create it. Alternatively, maybe the service exists but the metrics endpoint is on a different path or port, leading to scraping failure. \n\nSo, the next tool call should be to list all services in the namespace to check for the user-service service. If it's not present, that's the issue. If it is, they need to check the service's YAML to ensure it's correctly pointing to the pod's port and that the metrics endpoint is accessible. Also, checking the Prometheus configuration to ensure it's scraping the correct service and port would be necessary. But first, confirming the service existence is crucial.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get services -n social-network"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-a831ba706aff42b9', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 384, 'prompt_tokens': 6821, 'total_tokens': 7205, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc5e-4e38-75a3-b38c-4af9cb37d92b-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get services -n social-network'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-a831ba706aff42b9', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 6821, 'output_tokens': 384, 'total_tokens': 7205}), ToolMessage(content='NAME                     TYPE           CLUSTER-IP       EXTERNAL-IP                                PORT(S)     AGE\naux-service              ClusterIP      10.105.158.118   <none>                                     8080/TCP    112s\ncompose-post-service     ClusterIP      10.107.183.239   <none>                                     9090/TCP    5m37s\nhome-timeline-redis      ClusterIP      10.99.246.172    <none>                                     6379/TCP    5m37s\nhome-timeline-service    ClusterIP      10.100.85.68     <none>                                     9090/TCP    5m37s\njaeger                   Externa