s" but got no results. Wait, the service\'s name is "user-service", so maybe the job should be "user-service" instead of "user-service-metrics". Let me check the Prometheus configuration. But I can\'t access that directly. Alternatively, maybe the metrics endpoint is not exposed correctly. \n\nWait, the logs show the server started, but maybe the metrics endpoint isn\'t actually running. Or maybe the service is not properly configured to expose the metrics port. Let me check the service\'s YAML to see the target port and port mapping. But I can\'t do that now. Alternatively, maybe the container isn\'t exposing metrics on port 9090. The logs don\'t mention any metrics server. \n\nWait, the user-service might be using a different port for metrics. For example, sometimes services expose metrics on a different port, like 9100. If the service is configured to use port 9090 but the actual metrics are on another port, the scrape would fail. Let me check the service\'s configuration. But since I can\'t view the YAML, maybe I can use the get_services tool again to see if there\'s a service named "user-service-metrics" or something similar. Wait, the get_services tool returned a list that didn\'t include "user-service-metrics". \n\nAlternatively, maybe the Prometheus scrape configuration is pointing to the wrong port. If the service is on 9090 but Prometheus is configured to scrape port 9100, that would fail. But how can I check that? Without access to Prometheus\'s config, maybe I should try querying the metrics endpoint directly. \n\nWait, the user-service\'s pod is on IP 10.244.2.82, port 9090. If I could curl that endpoint, I could see if metrics are being served. But I don\'t have a tool for that. Alternatively, maybe the metrics are being exposed but not scraped because of a misconfiguration in Prometheus. \n\nWait, the initial get_metrics query for "up{job=\\"user-service\\"}" returned nothing. Maybe the job name is different. Let me try querying with the service name. Or maybe the metrics are not being exported at all. The logs don\'t show any errors related to metrics. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the user-service isn\'t exporting metrics. If the service is a custom application, it might not have a metrics endpoint. But the logs show the server started, so maybe it\'s a different issue. \n\nWait, the user-service\'s container is using the image "deathstarbench/social-network-microservices:latest". Maybe the image doesn\'t include a metrics exporter. If that\'s the case, then the metrics wouldn\'t be available. But the user-service is part of the social network, which likely has metrics. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the metrics are being exported but not properly labeled. For example, if the job name in the metrics is different from what\'s queried. \n\nI think the next step is to check the metrics endpoint directly. Since I can\'t do that, maybe I should check the service\'s configuration again. Wait, the service is ClusterIP, so it\'s only accessible within the cluster. Maybe the Prometheus server is not configured to scrape that service. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the user-service is not exposing metrics on the expected port. Let me check the container\'s ports again. The pod\'s container has port 9090/TCP, and the service is mapped to that port. So that should be correct. \n\nWait, maybe the metrics are being exported on a different path. For example, /metrics. If the Prometheus scrape job is configured to scrape /metrics, but the service is exporting on a different path, that would fail. But without access to the Prometheus config, I can\'t check that. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the user-service is not running the metrics exporter. If the application doesn\'t expose metrics, then the up{job="user-service"} query would return nothing. But the logs show the server started, so maybe the metrics are not being collected. \n\nI think the root cause is that the user-service is not exposing metrics, or the metrics are not being scraped by Prom