</tool_call> - base_agent.py:llm_thinking_step:68 [Full Prompt = [SystemMessage(content='Mitigate the identified faults in an IT incident.  Some or none of the microservices have faults.  Get all the pods and deployments to figure out what kind of services are running in the cluster if you don\'t know what the services are. You should carefully identify the whether the faults are present and if they are, what is the root cause of the fault. You can stop mitigation once you\'ve fixed all the faults. \nGo as deep as you can into what is causing the issue, and mitigate the fault.\nYour instructions to the tools must be clear and concise. Your queries to tools need to be single turn.\nRemember to check these, and remember this information: ## Workloads (Applications) - **Pod**: The smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes, representing a single instance of a running application. Can contain one or more tightly coupled containers. - **ReplicaSet**: Ensures that a specified number of pod replicas are running at all times. Often managed indirectly through Deployments. - **Deployment**: Manages the deployment and lifecycle of applications. Provides declarative updates for Pods and ReplicaSets. - **StatefulSet**: Manages stateful applications with unique pod identities and stable storage. Used for workloads like databases. - **DaemonSet**: Ensures that a copy of a specific pod runs on every node in the cluster. Useful for node monitoring agents, log collectors, etc. - **Job**: Manages batch processing tasks that are expected to complete successfully. Ensures pods run to completion. - **CronJob**: Schedules jobs to run at specified times or intervals (similar to cron in Linux).\n## Networking - **Service**: Provides a stable network endpoint for accessing a group of pods. Types: ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer, and ExternalName. - **Ingress**: Manages external HTTP(S) access to services in the cluster. Supports routing and load balancing for HTTP(S) traffic. - **NetworkPolicy**: Defines rules for network communication between pods and other entities. Used for security and traffic control.\n## Storage - **PersistentVolume (PV)**: Represents a piece of storage in the cluster, provisioned by an administrator or dynamically. - **PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC)**: Represents a request for storage by a user. Binds to a PersistentVolume. - **StorageClass**: Defines different storage tiers or backends for dynamic provisioning of PersistentVolumes. - **ConfigMap**: Stores configuration data as key-value pairs for applications. - **Secret**: Stores sensitive data like passwords, tokens, or keys in an encrypted format.\n## Configuration and Metadata - **Namespace**: Logical partitioning of resources within the cluster for isolation and organization. - **ConfigMap**: Provides non-sensitive configuration data in key-value format. - **Secret**: Stores sensitive configuration data securely. - **ResourceQuota**: Restricts resource usage (e.g., CPU, memory) within a namespace. - **LimitRange**: Enforces minimum and maximum resource limits for containers in a namespace.\n## Cluster Management - **Node**: Represents a worker machine in the cluster (virtual or physical). Runs pods and is managed by the control plane. - **ClusterRole and Role**: Define permissions for resources at the cluster or namespace level. - **ClusterRoleBinding and RoleBinding**: Bind roles to users or groups for authorization. - **ServiceAccount**: Associates processes in pods with permissions for accessing the Kubernetes API.\nAn example procedure to remediate the faults: 1) Formulate a remediation plan with a list of actionable steps. 2) Execute the plan, one step at a time. 3) Check if the plan execution worked as you desired in the IT environment. 4) If not, you can either call wait_tool to wait for it to take effect or take other actions. 5) Otherwise, continue the plan and execution process until you call submit_tool as you believe the application has become healthy.\nThe following is a detailed description of your tasks.\n1) mitigation: Mitigate the identified faults in an IT incident with the provided tools. You can submit an empty dict "ans" with the submit_tool as this task is not graded over your answer but the final result of the mitigation; therefore, you have to make sure the application has become healthy before you call submit_tool.\n', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='603b68e5-3226-49cc-b0bd-fcbe84e8ee5a'), HumanMessage(content='You will be working this application:\n\nSocial Network\n\nHere are some descriptions about the application:\n\nA social network with unidirectional follow relationships, implemented with loosely-coupled microservices, communicating with each other via Thrift RPCs.\n\nIt belongs to this namespace:\n\nsocial-network\n\nThe following is the information of faults identified by a diagnosis agent in the app:\n\n<think>\nOkay, let\'s try to figure out what\'s going on here. The user is dealing with a social network application made up of microservices, and they\'re trying to diagnose why the user-service isn\'t showing up in metrics. \n\nFirst, they checked the list of services in the cluster using get_services, which showed that user-service is present. Then they tried querying Prometheus for metrics using the job name "user-service" and "user-service-metrics," but both returned empty results. \n\nNext, they checked the pods and deployments, confirming that the user-service pod is running. The logs indicated the service started without errors, but no metrics were collected. They then checked the service configuration and found that the user-service has a ClusterIP with port 9090, which matches the container\'s port. \n\nBut the metrics still weren\'t showing up. The possible issues could be that the metrics exporter isn\'t running inside the container, or the Prometheus scrape job isn\'t configured correctly. Since the logs don\'t mention any metrics-related errors, it\'s likely the application isn\'t exporting metrics at all. Alternatively, the metrics might be on a different port or path than what Prometheus is scraping. \n\nThe user-service\'s container image might not include a metrics exporter, or the exporter might be misconfigured. Without access to the Prometheus configuration, it\'s hard to tell if the scrape job is set up correctly. However, the absence of metrics despite the service being up suggests that the metrics aren\'t being exposed, leading to the conclusion that the root cause is either a missing or misconfigured metrics exporter in the user-service container or an incorrect Prometheus scrape job configuration.\n</think>\n\nThe root cause of the failure is that the **user-service is not exposing metrics**, or the metrics are not being scraped by Prometheus. Despite the user-service pod being in a "Running" state and the service being correctly mapped to port 9090, no metrics are collected. This indicates:\n\n1. **Metrics Exporter Misconfiguration**: The user-service container may not include a metrics exporter (e.g., Prometheus) or the exporter is not properly configured to expose metrics on the expected port/path (e.g., `/metrics`).\n2. **Prometheus Scrape Job Issue**: The Prometheus scrape job for `user-service` might be misconfigured (e.g., incorrect port, path, or job name), preventing metrics from being scraped.\n3. **Application-Level Issue**: The application itself may not be designed to expose metrics, or the metrics endpoint is not active.\n\n**Recommendation**: Verify the metrics exporter configuration within the user-service container and ensure the Prometheus scrape job targets the correct endpoint (port, path, and job name). If the exporter is missing, integrate it into the container image or adjust the scrape configuration accordingly.\n\nIn each round, there is a thinking stage. In the thinking stage, you are given a list of tools. Think about what you want to call. Return your tool choice and the reasoning behind.\nWhen choosing the tool, refer to the tool by its name.\nThen, there is a tool-call stage, where you make a tool_call consistent with your explanation.\nYou can run up to 20 rounds to finish the tasks.\nIf you call submit_tool in tool-call stage, the process will end immediately.\nIf you exceed this limitation, the system will force you to make a submission.\nYou will begin by analyzing the service\'s state and telemetry with the tools.\n', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='90c035e8-555a-4c4e-84bd-c930668562b4'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Here are all the tools you can use:\ntool name: wait_tool\n\ntool descriptions Use this tool to wait for you action to take effect. The upper limit is 120 seconds. \n    Any value above 120 seconds will be truncated to 120 seconds. If you call this tool\n    along with other tools in your tool_calls list, this tool will be scheduled to the \n    last for execution.\n\n    Args:\n        seconds (int): Number of seconds to wait.\n\n\ntool name: get_traces\n\ntool description: Get Jaeger traces for a given service in the last n minutes.\n\n    Args:\n        service (str): The name of the service for which to retrieve trace data.\n        last_n_minutes (int): The time range (in minutes) to look back from the current time.\n\n\ntool name: get_services\n\ntool description: Retrieve the list of service names from the Grafana instance.\n\n    Args:\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A list of service names available in Grafana.\n\n\ntool name: get_operations\n\ntool description: Query available operations for a specific service from the Grafana instance.\n\n    Args:\n        service (str): The name of the service whose operations should be retrieved.\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A list of operation names associated with the specified service.\n\n\ntool name: get_dependency_graph\n\ntool description: Get service dependency graph from Jaeger\'s native dependencies API.\n    Args:\n        last_n_minutes (int): The time range (in minutes) to look back from the current time.\n    Returns:\n        str: JSON object representing the dependency graph.\n\n\ntool name: get_metrics\n\ntool description: Query real-time metrics data from the Prometheus instance.\n\n    Args:\n        query (str): A Prometheus Query Language (PromQL) expression used to fetch metric values.\n\n    Returns:\n        dict: The raw Prometheus response containing metric results, including timestamps, values, and labels.\n\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely\n\ntool description: This is a tool used to safely execute kubectl commands. Whatever commands you executed will be stored in a rollback \nmechanism which later is used to roll back the cluster state in case your current attempt fails.\n\n    Args:\n        command (str): The kubectl command you would like to execute, must start with "kubectl"\n        INTERACTIVE COMMANDS ARE NOT SUPPORTED, e.g. "kubectl edit" \n        INTERACTIVE FLAGS ARE NOT SUPPORTED, e.g., "kubectl logs -f"\n\n\ntool name: submit_tool\n\ntool description: The tool to submit benchmark results\n    \n    Args:\n        ans (str): the answer you would like to submit to the benchmark\n\n\nChoose a tool from the list and output the tool name. Justify your tool choice. In the next step, you will generate a tool call for this tool', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='b118f3ca-9f1a-4fed-8579-d7d4d9cd1f4d'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s start by understanding the problem. The user mentioned that the user-service isn\'t showing up in metrics. They checked the services with get_services and confirmed the service exists. Then they tried querying Prometheus with the job names "user-service" and "user-service-metrics," but got empty results. They also checked the pods and deployments, found the pod is running, and the logs don\'t show errors. The service configuration shows the correct port mapping.\n\nSo, the next step is to check if the metrics are actually being exposed by the user-service. Since the Prometheus query returned nothing, maybe the metrics endpoint isn\'t set up correctly. The user-service might not have a metrics exporter like Prometheus, or the exporter is on a different port or path. Alternatively, the Prometheus scrape job might not be configured to target the correct endpoint.\n\nTo investigate, I should first check the metrics that are available in Prometheus. Using the get_metrics tool with a query that targets the user-service\'s metrics endpoint could help. For example, if the metrics are exposed on port 9090, the query might be something like `up{job="user-service"}`. If this returns no data, it indicates that the metrics aren\'t being scraped. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the service isn\'t exporting metrics at all. In that case, the application might need a metrics exporter added to its container. But without knowing the exact setup, checking the existing metrics is a good first step. So, using get_metrics with the appropriate query would help determine if the metrics are available and if the scrape job is configured correctly.\n</think>\n\nget_metrics\n\nThe get_metrics tool will query Prometheus to check if metrics are being collected for the user-service. This will help confirm whether the metrics are exposed and being scraped correctly. If no metrics are returned, it indicates the service may not be exporting metrics or the scrape configuration is incorrect.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 386, 'prompt_tokens': 2242, 'total_tokens': 2628, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc5e-fb58-79e3-91f2-934ba8ab967f-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2242, 'output_tokens': 386, 'total_tokens': 2628}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='c72c3937-500a-4e53-b93b-cf28af368388'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to figure out why the user-service isn\'t showing up in metrics. They already checked the services list and confirmed the service exists. Then they tried querying Prometheus with the job names "user-service" and "user-service-metrics", but got empty results. Now they\'re using get_metrics to check if there are any metrics available.\n\nThe next step is to use the get_metrics tool with a specific query. The user mentioned the service is on port 9090, so maybe the metrics are on that port. But the job name might be different. Let me think about the correct PromQL query. If the service is exposed on port 9090, the query might be something like `up{job="user-service"}` to check if the service is up. Alternatively, if the metrics endpoint is `/metrics`, the query could be `scrape_duration_seconds{job="user-service"}`. But since the user previously tried "user-service" and "user-service-metrics" and got nothing, maybe the job name is different. \n\nWait, the user-service might not have a metrics endpoint at all. Or maybe the exporter isn\'t running. The get_metrics tool will help confirm if any metrics are being scraped. If the query returns nothing, that points to the metrics not being exposed. So the correct approach is to use get_metrics with a query that targets the user-service\'s metrics endpoint. Let\'s use `up{job="user-service"}` to check if the service is considered up by Prometheus. If that returns no data, then the issue is either the exporter isn\'t running or the scrape job isn\'t configured correctly. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the metrics are on a different port. The user mentioned the service is mapped to port 9090, so the scrape job might be configured to scrape that port. But if the exporter isn\'t there, nothing will be collected. So the query should check for the presence of metrics from that job. Let\'s proceed with the query `up{job="user-service"}` to see if any instances are up. If there\'s no data, that\'s a clear sign the metrics aren\'t being scraped. Then the next step would be to check the service\'s configuration or the Prometheus scrape jobs.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"query": "up{job=\\"user-service\\"}"}', 'name': 'get_metrics'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-962e017fb644ec85', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 500, 'prompt_tokens': 3325, 'total_tokens': 3825, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc5f-0d6a-7ac0-ba47-6bd8ebceaf3b-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'get_metrics', 'args': {'query': 'up{job="user-service"}'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-962e017fb644ec85', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3325, 'output_tokens': 500, 'total_tokens': 3825}), ToolMessage(content='("{\'resultType\': \'vector\', \'result\': []}", 12)', id='7b3b7b1a-5da8-4d94-9b4a-a21bc12e87d1', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-962e017fb644ec85'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='65f7d225-4e21-476d-bb3a-6fb85fe9eff9'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to figure out why the user-service isn\'t showing up in metrics. They used get_metrics with the query "up{job=\\"user-service\\"}" and got an empty result. That means Prometheus isn\'t picking up any metrics from that job.\n\nHmm, so the next step is to check if the service is actually exposing metrics. Maybe the metrics endpoint isn\'t running or isn\'t accessible. The user-service pod is running, but maybe the container isn\'t starting the metrics server. Or perhaps the port is wrong. \n\nWait, the user checked the service configuration and found that the service is mapped to port 9090. But maybe the metrics are on a different port. Or the exporter isn\'t configured correctly. Alternatively, the Prometheus scrape job might not be set up to scrape that port. \n\nI should check the pods to see if the metrics server is running. Using exec_kubectl_cmd_safely to check the container logs or see if the metrics server process is active. For example, checking the logs of the user-service pod to see if there are any errors related to the metrics endpoint. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the metrics are exposed but the scrape job isn\'t configured correctly. The user might need to check the Prometheus configuration for the user-service job. But since the user can\'t directly access Prometheus config, maybe they can check the scrape targets. \n\nWait, the get_services tool can list services in Grafana, but the user already knows the service exists. Maybe checking the deployment to see if the container is set up to expose metrics. For example, checking the Dockerfile or the deployment YAML to see if the metrics exporter is included. \n\nBut the user can\'t modify the deployment directly yet. So maybe the next step is to check the pod\'s container ports to ensure that the metrics port is correctly exposed. Using kubectl describe pod to check the ports. \n\nAlternatively, using exec_kubectl_cmd_safely to run a command inside the pod to check if the metrics server is running. For example, checking if the Prometheus exporter is running on port 9090. \n\nWait, the user already confirmed the service is mapped to port 9090. So maybe the metrics are on a different path, like /metrics, but the scrape job isn\'t configured to look there. Or the service isn\'t exposing the metrics endpoint at all. \n\nAnother approach: check the logs of the user-service pod to see if there are any errors related to the metrics. If the metrics server isn\'t running, the logs might show that. \n\nSo the next tool should be exec_kubectl_cmd_safely to check the logs of the user-service pod. That way, we can see if there are any issues with the metrics server or if the container is failing to start the exporter. \n\nAlternatively, check the container\'s processes to see if the metrics server is running. For example, using ps or netstat inside the container. \n\nBut the user can\'t run interactive commands, so they have to use kubectl exec to run a command. So the command would be something like "curl http://localhost:9090/metrics" to check if the metrics endpoint is accessible. \n\nWait, but the user might not have curl installed. Alternatively, check the logs for any errors related to the metrics server. \n\nSo the plan is to use exec_kubectl_cmd_safely to check the logs of the user-service pod for any errors related to the metrics exporter. If the logs show that the exporter isn\'t running, that\'s a clue. If there are no errors, maybe the exporter isn\'t configured. \n\nAlternatively, check the container\'s processes to see if the metrics server is running. \n\nSo the next tool call should be exec_kubectl_cmd_safely with the command to check the logs or processes of the user-service pod.\n</think>\n\nexec_kubectl_cmd_safely\n\nThe exec_kubectl_cmd_safely tool will allow us to inspect the user-service pod\'s logs or check if the metrics server is running inside the container. This will help determine if the metrics exporter is active or if there are errors preventing metric exposure. For example, we can check the logs for errors or verify if the metrics server process is running.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 876, 'prompt_tokens': 2403, 'total_tokens': 3279, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc5f-2640-7b50-bb50-0aa4cc7d45f4-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2403, 'output_tokens': 876, 'total_tokens': 3279}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='d8557296-cfde-4032-b20d-c4b38e693367'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to figure out why the user-service isn't showing up in metrics. They already checked with get_metrics and got an empty result. Now they want to use exec_kubectl_cmd_safely to look into the pod.\n\nFirst, I need to determine what command to run inside the pod. The goal is to check if the metrics exporter is running. Common metrics exporters like Prometheus often run on port 9090. So, checking the container's processes might help. The command would be something like checking the running processes with ps or looking at the logs with journalctl. But since the user mentioned the container's port 9090 is mapped, maybe the exporter is supposed to be on that port. \n\nWait, the user-service pod is running, but maybe the metrics endpoint isn't accessible. Alternatively, the container might not have the exporter installed. So, checking the logs for any errors related to the metrics server would be useful. The command could be kubectl logs <pod-name> to view the logs. But the pod name isn't specified yet. Earlier, they checked the pods and deployments, so maybe the pod name is user-service or similar. \n\nAlternatively, they could check the container's processes to see if the metrics server is running. Using kubectl exec to run a command inside the container. For example, ps aux to list processes. If the metrics server isn't listed, that's a problem. Or, if the exporter is a sidecar container, maybe it's not running. \n\nBut the user needs to specify the pod name. Since they know the service is user-service, maybe the pod is named user-service-<randomstring>. So, first, they might need to get the pod name. But the user already confirmed the pod is running. Maybe they can use kubectl get pods in the social-network namespace to find the exact name. However, the previous step mentioned that the user-service pod is running, so perhaps the pod name is known or can be inferred. \n\nAlternatively, the user could check the container's files to see if the metrics exporter is present. For example, checking if there's a binary like prometheus or a script that starts the exporter. But that might be more involved. \n\nGiven the tools available, the exec_kubectl_cmd_safely can run commands like logs or check processes. The most straightforward step is to check the logs for any errors related to the metrics server. So, the command would be kubectl logs <pod-name> for the user-service pod. But since the pod name isn't known, maybe the user can use kubectl get pods in the social-network namespace to find it. However, the user might not have done that yet. \n\nWait, in the previous steps, the user checked the pods and deployments, so maybe they already have the pod name. Let me check the history. The user ran get_services, which showed user-service is present. Then they checked the pods and deployments, confirming the user-service pod is running. So, the pod name might be user-service or similar. \n\nAssuming the pod name is user-service, the command would be kubectl logs user-service -n social-network. But if the pod has a different name, like user-service-xyz, that might not work. However, without the exact pod name, this could be an issue. Alternatively, using kubectl logs to get the logs of the pod, even if the name isn't exact, but that's risky. \n\nAlternatively, check the processes inside the container. For example, kubectl exec -it <pod-name> -- ps aux to see if the metrics server is running. If the exporter is a sidecar, maybe it's running on a different container. \n\nBut given the uncertainty, the safest bet is to check the logs for any errors. So, the command would be to get the logs of the user-service pod. If the pod name isn't known, maybe the user can first get the pod name with kubectl get pods -n social-network. But the user might not have done that yet. \n\nWait, the user already confirmed the pod is running, so maybe they can proceed to check the logs. Let's proceed with that. The command would be kubectl logs user-service -n social-network. If that's not possible, maybe the pod name is different, but the user can adjust accordingly. \n\nSo, the tool call would be exec_kubectl_cmd_safely with the command to get the logs of the user-service pod in the social-network namespace.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl logs user-service -n social-network"}', 'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-bd2287adbc74a2d8', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 971, 'prompt_tokens': 3507, 'total_tokens': 4478, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc5f-4e80-7941-9390-f98e5913d17e-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl logs user-service -n social-network'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-bd2287adbc74a2d8', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3507, 'output_tokens': 971, 'total_tokens': 4478}), ToolMessage(content='Command Rejected: Error executing kubectl command:\nerror: error from server (NotFound): pods "user-service" not found in namespace "social-network"\n', id='22325711-1d69-46a1-93cf-960fcb181b3b', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-bd2287adbc74a2d8'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='adf30b11-1c62-4ef2-be9b-9c5421e989ce'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, the user tried to get the logs for the user-service pod but got a "Not Found" error. That means the pod isn\'t running or doesn\'t exist. Let me check why.\n\nFirst, maybe the pod name is different. The user might have used "user-service" as the pod name, but in Kubernetes, the pod name is usually the same as the deployment or replica set name. Wait, earlier they checked the deployments and found that the user-service is a Deployment. So the pod name might be something like user-service-<hash> or similar. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the deployment isn\'t running. Let me check the status of the deployment. Using kubectl get deployments in the social-network namespace would show if the deployment is active and if the pods are being created. If the deployment is not in a Ready state, that could explain why the pod isn\'t found. \n\nAnother possibility is that the service is misconfigured. The user-service might be pointing to the wrong image or has incorrect environment variables. Checking the deployment\'s YAML could help. Also, if the metrics exporter isn\'t included in the container image, the metrics wouldn\'t be available. \n\nWait, the user tried to get the logs but the pod wasn\'t found. So the first step is to verify if the deployment is running. Let me use the exec_kubectl_cmd_safely tool to check the deployments and pods in the namespace. That way, I can see the current state of the user-service deployment and any associated pods. If the deployment is not in a Ready state, that\'s a problem. If the pods are not running, maybe there\'s an issue with the container image or resources. \n\nSo the next tool call should be to get the deployments and pods in the social-network namespace. That will provide more context on why the pod isn\'t found and help identify the root cause.\n</think>\n\nexec_kubectl_cmd_safely\n\nThe exec_kubectl_cmd_safely tool will check the status of the user-service deployment and pods in the social-network namespace. This will confirm if the deployment is active, if pods are running, or if there are errors in the deployment configuration (e.g., image pull failures, resource limits, or misconfigured environment variables). \n\n<tool_call>\n{"name": "exec_kubectl_cmd_safely", "arguments": {"command": "kubectl get deployments,pods -n social-network"}}\n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 492, 'prompt_tokens': 2604, 'total_tokens': 3096, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc5f-7e9c-7b02-8798-931ccce2ea37-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2604, 'output_tokens': 492, 'total_tokens': 3096}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='19860804-2f7f-447f-94c1-26c69f2cd6ec'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to troubleshoot the user-service in their social network application. They\'ve already checked the metrics with get_metrics, which returned nothing, and tried to get the logs but got a pod not found error. Now they\'re using exec_kubectl_cmd_safely to get deployments and pods in the social-network namespace.\n\nThe response from the tool call was a command rejection because the pod "user-service" wasn\'t found. Wait, but the user ran "kubectl get deployments,pods -n social-network", which should list both deployments and pods. Maybe the pod name is different. The initial error was about the pod not being found, but perhaps the deployment exists, and the pods are just not named "user-service". \n\nSo, the next step is to check the exact names of the deployments and pods. Maybe the deployment is named something like "user-service-deployment" and the pods are named with a prefix or suffix. The user needs to get the correct pod names to check their status and logs. \n\nTherefore, the next tool call should be to get the list of deployments and pods again, possibly with more details. Alternatively, check the deployment\'s status to see if it\'s rolling out and what pods are associated with it. If the deployment is not in a ready state, that could explain why the pod isn\'t found or isn\'t running. \n\nAnother possibility is that the service is not properly linked to the pods, or there\'s a typo in the service name. But since the user already confirmed the service exists via get_services, the focus should be on the deployment and pods. \n\nSo, the next action is to retrieve the deployment details to see the pod names and their current status. Using kubectl get deployment <deployment-name> -n social-network would show the replicas and pod names. If the deployment isn\'t running, checking the events or logs of the deployment could provide more insight. \n\nAlternatively, using kubectl get all -n social-network would list all resources, including pods, deployments, services, etc., which might help identify any issues with the deployment or pods. But since the user already tried get deployments,pods, maybe they need to check the specific deployment\'s pod names. \n\nIn any case, the key is to find the correct pod names associated with the user-service deployment to check their status and logs. Once the correct pod names are known, the user can then check the logs or describe the pods to see if there are any errors or issues preventing them from running properly.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get all -n social-network"}', 'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-a2fac8e033972c32', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 549, 'prompt_tokens': 3736, 'total_tokens': 4285, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc5f-9550-7111-8a8a-62b53bd13d6f-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get all -n social-network'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-a2fac8e033972c32', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3736, 'output_tokens': 549, 'total_tokens': 4285}), ToolMessage(content='Command Rejected: Error executing kubectl command:\nError from server (Forbidden): replicationcontrollers is forbidden: User "system:serviceaccount:sregym:mcp-server" cannot list resource "replicationcontrollers" in API group "" in the namespace "social-network"\n', id='b8f0cf98-0788-44c0-90a1-a89cd339b9ce', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-a2fac8e033972c32'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='73d0e8dc-cf5d-41d9-a9dd-b6a2d47925ce')]]
2026-03-17 15:17:44 - DEBUG - all.stratus.mitigation - [Loop 3] Inject tool call prompt (repeated) - base_agent.py:llm_tool_call_prompt_inject_step:85
2026-03-17 15:17:47 - DEBUG - all.stratus.mitigation - [Loop 3] Tool call - base_agent.py:llm_tool_call_step:102 [Full Prompt = [SystemMessage(content='Mitigate the identified faults in an IT incident.  Some or none of the microservices have faults.  Get all the pods and deployments to figure out what kind of services are running in the cluster if you don\'t know what the services are. You should carefully identify the whether the faults are present and if they are, what is the root cause of the fault. You can stop mitigation once you\'ve fixed all the faults. \nGo as deep as you can into what is causing the issue, and mitigate the fault.\nYour instructions to the tools must be clear and concise. Your queries to tools need to be single turn.\nRemember to check these, and remember this information: ## Workloads (Applications) - **Pod**: The smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes, representing a single instance of a running application. Can contain one or more tightly coupled containers. - **ReplicaSet**: Ensures that a specified number of pod replicas are running at all times. Often managed indirectly through Deployments. - **Deployment**: Manages the deployment and lifecycle of applications. Provides declarative updates for Pods and ReplicaSets. - **StatefulSet**: Manages stateful applications with unique pod identities and stable storage. Used for workloads like databases. - **DaemonSet**: Ensures that a copy of a specific pod runs on every node in the cluster. Useful for node monitoring agents, log collectors, etc. - **Job**: Manages batch processing tasks that are expected to complete successfully. Ensures pods run to completion. - **CronJob**: Schedules jobs to run at specified times or intervals (similar to cron in Linux).\n## Networking - **Service**: Provides a stable network endpoint for accessing a group of pods. Types: ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer, and ExternalName. - **Ingress**: Manages external HTTP(S) access to services in the cluster. Supports routing and load balancing for HTTP(S) traffic. - **NetworkPolicy**: Defines rules for network communication between pods and other entities. Used for security and traffic control.\n## Storage - **PersistentVolume (PV)**: Represents a piece of storage in the cluster, provisioned by an administrator or dynamically. - **PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC)**: Represents a request for storage by a user. Binds to a PersistentVolume. - **StorageClass**: Defines different storage tiers or backends for dynamic provisioning of PersistentVolumes. - **ConfigMap**: Stores configuration data as key-value pairs for applications. - **Secret**: Stores sensitive data like passwords, tokens, or keys in an encrypted format.\n## Configuration and Metadata - **Namespace**: Logical partitioning of resources within the cluster for isolation and organization. - **ConfigMap**: Provides non-sensitive configuration data in key-value format. - **Secret**: Stores sensitive configuration data securely. - **ResourceQuota**: Restricts resource usage (e.g., CPU, memory) within a namespace. - **LimitRange**: Enforces minimum and maximum resource limits for containers in a namespace.\n## Cluster Management - **Node**: Represents a worker machine in the cluster (virtual or physical). Runs pods and is managed by the control plane. - **ClusterRole and Role**: Define permissions for resources at the cluster or namespace level. - **ClusterRoleBinding and RoleBinding**: Bind roles to users or groups for authorization. - **ServiceAccount**: Associates processes in pods with permissions for accessing the Kubernetes API.\nAn example procedure to remediate the faults: 1) Formulate a remediation plan with a list of actionable steps. 2) Execute the plan, one step at a time. 3) Check if the plan execution worked as you desired in the IT environment. 4) If not, you can either call wait_tool to wait for it to take effect or take other actions. 5) Otherwise, continue the plan and execution process until you call submit_tool as you believe the application has become healthy.\nThe following is a detailed description of your tasks.\n1) mitigation: Mitigate the identified faults in an IT incident with the provided tools. You can submit an empty dict "ans" with the submit_tool as this task is not graded over your answer but the final result of the mitigation; therefore, you have to make sure the application has become healthy before you call submit_tool.\n', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='603b68e5-3226-49cc-b0bd-fcbe84e8ee5a'), HumanMessage(content='You will be working this application:\n\nSocial Network\n\nHere are some descriptions about the application:\n\nA social network with unidirectional follow relationships, implemented with loosely-coupled microservices, communicating with each other via Thrift RPCs.\n\nIt belongs to this namespace:\n\nsocial-network\n\nThe following is the information of faults identified by a diagnosis agent in the app:\n\n<think>\nOkay, let\'s try to figure out what\'s going on here. The user is dealing with a social network application made up of microservices, and they\'re trying to diagnose why the user-service isn\'t showing up in metrics. \n\nFirst, they checked the list of services in the cluster using get_services, which showed that user-service is present. Then they tried querying Prometheus for metrics using the job name "user-service" and "user-service-metrics," but both returned empty results. \n\nNext, they checked the pods and deployments, confirming that the user-service pod is running. The logs indicated the service started without errors, but no metrics were collected. They then checked the service configuration and found that the user-service has a ClusterIP with port 9090, which matches the container\'s port. \n\nBut the metrics still weren\'t showing up. The possible issues could be that the metrics exporter isn\'t running inside the container, or the Prometheus scrape job isn\'t configured correctly. Since the logs don\'t mention any metrics-related errors, it\'s likely the application isn\'t exporting metrics at all. Alternatively, the metrics might be on a different port or path than what Prometheus is scraping. \n\nThe user-service\'s container image might not include a metrics exporter, or the exporter might be misconfigured. Without access to the Prometheus configuration, it\'s hard to tell if the scrape job is set up correctly. However, the absence of metrics despite the service being up suggests that the metrics aren\'t being exposed, leading to the conclusion that the root cause is either a missing or misconfigured metrics exporter in the user-service container or an incorrect Prometheus scrape job configuration.\n</think>\n\nThe root cause of the failure is that the **user-service is not exposing metrics**, or the metrics are not being scraped by Prometheus. Despite the user-service pod being in a "Running" state and the service being correctly mapped to port 9090, no metrics are collected. This indicates:\n\n1. **Metrics Exporter Misconfiguration**: The user-service container may not include a metrics exporter (e.g., Prometheus) or the exporter is not properly configured to expose metrics on the expected port/path (e.g., `/metrics`).\n2. **Prometheus Scrape Job Issue**: The Prometheus scrape job for `user-service` might be misconfigured (e.g., incorrect port, path, or job name), preventing metrics from being scraped.\n3. **Application-Level Issue**: The application itself may not be designed to expose metrics, or the metrics endpoint is not active.\n\n**Recommendation**: Verify the metrics exporter configuration within the user-service container and ensure the Prometheus scrape job targets the correct endpoint (port, path, and job name). If the exporter is missing, integrate it into the container image or adjust the scrape configuration accordingly.\n\nIn each round, there is a thinking stage. In the thinking stage, you are given a list of tools. Think about what you want to call. Return your tool choice and the reasoning behind.\nWhen choosing the tool, refer to the tool by its name.\nThen, there is a tool-call stage, where you make a tool_call consistent with your explanation.\nYou can run up to 20 rounds to finish the tasks.\nIf you call submit_tool in tool-call stage, the process will end immediately.\nIf you exceed this limitation, the system will force you to make a submission.\nYou will begin by analyzing the service\'s state and telemetry with the tools.\n', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='90c035e8-555a-4c4e-84bd-c930668562b4'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Here are all the tools you can use:\ntool name: wait_tool\n\ntool descriptions Use this tool to wait for you action to take effect. The upper limit is 120 seconds. \n    Any value above 120 seconds will be truncated to 120 seconds. If you call this tool\n    along with other tools in your tool_calls list, this tool will be scheduled to the \n    last for execution.\n\n    Args:\n        seconds (int): Number of seconds to wait.\n\n\ntool name: get_traces\n\ntool description: Get Jaeger traces for a given service in the last n minutes.\n\n    Args:\n        service (str): The name of the service for which to retrieve trace data.\n        last_n_minutes (int): The time range (in minutes) to look back from the current time.\n\n\ntool name: get_services\n\ntool description: Retrieve the list of service names from the Grafana instance.\n\n    Args:\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A list of service names available in Grafana.\n\n\ntool name: get_operations\n\ntool description: Query available operations for a specific service from the Grafana instance.\n\n    Args:\n        service (str): The name of the service whose operations should be retrieved.\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A list of operation names associated with the specified service.\n\n\ntool name: get_dependency_graph\n\ntool description: Get service dependency graph from Jaeger\'s native dependencies API.\n    Args:\n        last_n_minutes (int): The time range (in minutes) to look back from the current time.\n    Returns:\n        str: JSON object representing the dependency graph.\n\n\ntool name: get_metrics\n\ntool description: Query real-time metrics data from the Prometheus instance.\n\n    Args:\n        query (str): A Prometheus Query Language (PromQL) expression used to fetch metric values.\n\n    Returns:\n        dict: The raw Prometheus response containing metric results, including timestamps, values, and labels.\n\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely\n\ntool description: This is a tool used to safely execute kubectl commands. Whatever commands you executed will be stored in a rollback \nmechanism which later is used to roll back the cluster state in case your current attempt fails.\n\n    Args:\n        command (str): The kubectl command you would like to execute, must start with "kubectl"\n        INTERACTIVE COMMANDS ARE NOT SUPPORTED, e.g. "kubectl edit" \n        INTERACTIVE FLAGS ARE NOT SUPPORTED, e.g., "kubectl logs -f"\n\n\ntool name: submit_tool\n\ntool description: The tool to submit benchmark results\n    \n    Args:\n        ans (str): the answer you would like to submit to the benchmark\n\n\nChoose a tool from the list and output the tool name. Justify your tool choice. In the next step, you will generate a tool call for this tool', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='b118f3ca-9f1a-4fed-8579-d7d4d9cd1f4d'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s start by understanding the problem. The user mentioned that the user-service isn\'t showing up in metrics. They checked the services with get_services and confirmed the service exists. Then they tried querying Prometheus with the job names "user-service" and "user-service-metrics," but got empty results. They also checked the pods and deployments, found the pod is running, and the logs don\'t show errors. The service configuration shows the correct port mapping.\n\nSo, the next step is to check if the metrics are actually being exposed by the user-service. Since the Prometheus query returned nothing, maybe the metrics endpoint isn\'t set up correctly. The user-service might not have a metrics exporter like Prometheus, or the exporter is on a different port or path. Alternatively, the Prometheus scrape job might not be configured to target the correct endpoint.\n\nTo investigate, I should first check the metrics that are available in Prometheus. Using the get_metrics tool with a query that targets the user-service\'s metrics endpoint could help. For example, if the metrics are exposed on port 9090, the query might be something like `up{job="user-service"}`. If this returns no data, it indicates that the metrics aren\'t being scraped. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the service isn\'t exporting metrics at all. In that case, the application might need a metrics exporter added to its container. But without knowing the exact setup, checking the existing metrics is a good first step. So, using get_metrics with the appropriate query would help determine if the metrics are available and if the scrape job is configured correctly.\n</think>\n\nget_metrics\n\nThe get_metrics tool will query Prometheus to check if metrics are being collected for the user-service. This will help confirm whether the metrics are exposed and being scraped correctly. If no metrics are returned, it indicates the service may not be exporting metrics or the scrape configuration is incorrect.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 386, 'prompt_tokens': 2242, 'total_tokens': 2628, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc5e-fb58-79e3-91f2-934ba8ab967f-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2242, 'output_tokens': 386, 'total_tokens': 2628}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='c72c3937-500a-4e53-b93b-cf28af368388'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to figure out why the user-service isn\'t showing up in metrics. They already checked the services list and confirmed the service exists. Then they tried querying Prometheus with the job names "user-service" and "user-service-metrics", but got empty results. Now they\'re using get_metrics to check if there are any metrics available.\n\nThe next step is to use the get_metrics tool with a specific query. The user mentioned the service is on port 9090, so maybe the metrics are on that port. But the job name might be different. Let me think about the correct PromQL query. If the service is exposed on port 9090, the query might be something like `up{job="user-service"}` to check if the service is up. Alternatively, if the metrics endpoint is `/metrics`, the query could be `scrape_duration_seconds{job="user-service"}`. But since the user previously tried "user-service" and "user-service-metrics" and got nothing, maybe the job name is different. \n\nWait, the user-service might not have a metrics endpoint at all. Or maybe the exporter isn\'t running. The get_metrics tool will help confirm if any metrics are being scraped. If the query returns nothing, that points to the metrics not being exposed. So the correct approach is to use get_metrics with a query that targets the user-service\'s metrics endpoint. Let\'s use `up{job="user-service"}` to check if the service is considered up by Prometheus. If that returns no data, then the issue is either the exporter isn\'t running or the scrape job isn\'t configured correctly. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the metrics are on a different port. The user mentioned the service is mapped to port 9090, so the scrape job might be configured to scrape that port. But if the exporter isn\'t there, nothing will be collected. So the query should check for the presence of metrics from that job. Let\'s proceed with the query `up{job="user-service"}` to see if any instances are up. If there\'s no data, that\'s a clear sign the metrics aren\'t being scraped. Then the next step would be to check the service\'s configuration or the Prometheus scrape jobs.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"query": "up{job=\\"user-service\\"}"}', 'name': 'get_metrics'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-962e017fb644ec85', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 500, 'prompt_tokens': 3325, 'total_tokens': 3825, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc5f-0d6a-7ac0-ba47-6bd8ebceaf3b-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'get_metrics', 'args': {'query': 'up{job="user-service"}'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-962e017fb644ec85', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3325, 'output_tokens': 500, 'total_tokens': 3825}), ToolMessage(content='("{\'resultType\': \'vector\', \'result\': []}", 12)', id='7b3b7b1a-5da8-4d94-9b4a-a21bc12e87d1', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-962e017fb644ec85'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='65f7d225-4e21-476d-bb3a-6fb85fe9eff9'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to figure out why the user-service isn\'t showing up in metrics. They used get_metrics with the query "up{job=\\"user-service\\"}" and got an empty result. That means Prometheus isn\'t picking up any metrics from that job.\n\nHmm, so the next step is to check if the service is actually exposing metrics. Maybe the metrics endpoint isn\'t running or isn\'t accessible. The user-service pod is running, but maybe the container isn\'t starting the metrics server. Or perhaps the port is wrong. \n\nWait, the user checked the service configuration and found that the service is mapped to port 9090. But maybe the metrics are on a different port. Or the exporter isn\'t configured correctly. Alternatively, the Prometheus scrape job might not be set up to scrape that port. \n\nI should check the pods to see if the metrics server is running. Using exec_kubectl_cmd_safely to check the container logs or see if the metrics server process is active. For example, checking the logs of the user-service pod to see if there are any errors related to the metrics endpoint. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the metrics are exposed but the scrape job isn\'t configured correctly. The user might need to check the Prometheus configuration for the user-service job. But since the user can\'t directly access Prometheus config, maybe they can check the scrape targets. \n\nWait, the get_services tool can list services in Grafana, but the user already knows the service exists. Maybe checking the deployment to see if the container is set up to expose metrics. For example, checking the Dockerfile or the deployment YAML to see if the metrics exporter is included. \n\nBut the user can\'t modify the deployment directly yet. So maybe the next step is to check the pod\'s container ports to ensure that the metrics port is correctly exposed. Using kubectl describe pod to check the ports. \n\nAlternatively, using exec_kubectl_cmd_safely to run a command inside the pod to check if the metrics server is running. For example, checking if the Prometheus exporter is running on port 9090. \n\nWait, the user already confirmed the service is mapped to port 9090. So maybe the metrics are on a different path, like /metrics, but the scrape job isn\'t configured to look there. Or the service isn\'t exposing the metrics endpoint at all. \n\nAnother approach: check the logs of the user-service pod to see if there are any errors related to the metrics. If the metrics server isn\'t running, the logs might show that. \n\nSo the next tool should be exec_kubectl_cmd_safely to check the logs of the user-service pod. That way, we can see if there are any issues with the metrics server or if the container is failing to start the exporter. \n\nAlternatively, check the container\'s processes to see if the metrics server is running. For example, using ps or netstat inside the container. \n\nBut the user can\'t run interactive commands, so they have to use kubectl exec to run a command. So the command would be something like "curl http://localhost:9090/metrics" to check if the metrics endpoint is accessible. \n\nWait, but the user might not have curl installed. Alternatively, check the logs for any errors related to the metrics server. \n\nSo the plan is to use exec_kubectl_cmd_safely to check the logs of the user-service pod for any errors related to the metrics exporter. If the logs show that the exporter isn\'t running, that\'s a clue. If there are no errors, maybe the exporter isn\'t configured. \n\nAlternatively, check the container\'s processes to see if the metrics server is running. \n\nSo the next tool call should be exec_kubectl_cmd_safely with the command to check the logs or processes of the user-service pod.\n</think>\n\nexec_kubectl_cmd_safely\n\nThe exec_kubectl_cmd_safely tool will allow us to inspect the user-service pod\'s logs or check if the metrics server is running inside the container. This will help determine if the metrics exporter is active or if there are errors preventing metric exposure. For example, we can check the logs for errors or verify if the metrics server process is running.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 876, 'prompt_tokens': 2403, 'total_tokens': 3279, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc5f-2640-7b50-bb50-0aa4cc7d45f4-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2403, 'output_tokens': 876, 'total_tokens': 3279}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='d8557296-cfde-4032-b20d-c4b38e693367'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to figure out why the user-service isn't showing up in metrics. They already checked with get_metrics and got an empty result. Now they want to use exec_kubectl_cmd_safely to look into the pod.\n\nFirst, I need to determine what command to run inside the pod. The goal is to check if the metrics exporter is running. Common metrics exporters like Prometheus often run on port 9090. So, checking the container's processes might help. The command would be something like checking the running processes with ps or looking at the logs with journalctl. But since the user mentioned the container's port 9090 is mapped, maybe the exporter is supposed to be on that port. \n\nWait, the user-service pod is running, but maybe the metrics endpoint isn't accessible. Alternatively, the container might not have the exporter installed. So, checking the logs for any errors related to the metrics server would be useful. The command could be kubectl logs <pod-name> to view the logs. But the pod name isn't specified yet. Earlier, they checked the pods and deployments, so maybe the pod name is user-service or similar. \n\nAlternatively, they could check the container's processes to see if the metrics server is running. Using kubectl exec to run a command inside the container. For example, ps aux to list processes. If the metrics server isn't listed, that's a problem. Or, if the exporter is a sidecar container, maybe it's not running. \n\nBut the user needs to specify the pod name. Since they know the service is user-service, maybe the pod is named user-service-<randomstring>. So, first, they might need to get the pod name. But the user already confirmed the pod is running. Maybe they can use kubectl get pods in the social-network namespace to find the exact name. However, the previous step mentioned that the user-service pod is running, so perhaps the pod name is known or can be inferred. \n\nAlternatively, the user could check the container's files to see if the metrics exporter is present. For example, checking if there's a binary like prometheus or a script that starts the exporter. But that might be more involved. \n\nGiven the tools available, the exec_kubectl_cmd_safely can run commands like logs or check processes. The most straightforward step is to check the logs for any errors related to the metrics server. So, the command would be kubectl logs <pod-name> for the user-service pod. But since the pod name isn't known, maybe the user can use kubectl get pods in the social-network namespace to find it. However, the user might not have done that yet. \n\nWait, in the previous steps, the user checked the pods and deployments, so maybe they already have the pod name. Let me check the history. The user ran get_services, which showed user-service is present. Then they checked the pods and deployments, confirming the user-service pod is running. So, the pod name might be user-service or similar. \n\nAssuming the pod name is user-service, the command would be kubectl logs user-service -n social-network. But if the pod has a different name, like user-service-xyz, that might not work. However, without the exact pod name, this could be an issue. Alternatively, using kubectl logs to get the logs of the pod, even if the name isn't exact, but that's risky. \n\nAlternatively, check the processes inside the container. For example, kubectl exe