hat the service is using Thrift RPCs for communication between microservices, but that doesn\'t necessarily mean they\'re using Prometheus for metrics. \n\nSo the root cause is likely that the user-service isn\'t exporting metrics. The solution would be to add a metrics exporter to the user-service container. For example, they could use the Prometheus client library to expose metrics on a specific port, like 9090. Then, they\'d need to configure Prometheus to scrape that endpoint. \n\nBut how do they do that? They might need to modify the deployment YAML for user-service to include the exporter as a sidecar container or as part of the main container. They\'d also need to update the Prometheus scrape configuration to include the new job for user-service, specifying the correct port and path. \n\nAlternatively, if the user-service is already using an exporter but it\'s not configured correctly, they might need to check the exporter\'s settings. For instance, the exporter might be running on a different port or path than what\'s specified in Prometheus. \n\nAnother possibility is that the metrics are being exposed but not being scraped due to a misconfiguration in Prometheus. The user might need to check the Prometheus configuration to ensure the job name and labels match what\'s expected. \n\nIn any case, the key steps are: confirm that the user-service is exporting metrics, check the Prometheus configuration, and ensure that the scrape job is correctly set up to collect those metrics. If the exporter isn\'t present, adding it would be the next step.\n</think>\n\nsubmit_tool\n{"ans": "The user-service is not exposing metrics. The logs confirm the service is running, but no metrics exporter is detected. Metrics are not being scraped by Prometheus, likely due to missing exporter configuration in the container or incorrect Prometheus scrape job settings. To resolve, ensure the user-service includes a metrics exporter (e.g., Prometheus) and verify the scrape job configura