ta={'input_tokens': 7247, 'output_tokens': 441, 'total_tokens': 7688}), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='504921d9-8c74-4f0b-a157-aa30f1b31f66'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s recap what\'s been done so far. The user is dealing with a social network application where the user-service isn\'t showing up in metrics. They checked the services, deployments, pods, and logs, but the main issue is that no metrics are being collected by Prometheus.\n\nFirst, they used get_services to list all services, which included user-service. Then, they tried get_metrics with the query "up{job=\\"user-service\\"}" but got an empty result. That suggests that either the service isn\'t exporting metrics or the Prometheus job isn\'t configured correctly.\n\nNext, they tried checking the pods and deployments, confirming the user-service pod is running. The logs showed the service started without errors, but no mention of metrics. They also checked the service configuration, confirming the port matches. However, the metrics still weren\'t there.\n\nThe user then tried several tools like get_dependency_graph, get_operations, and get_traces, but those didn\'t reveal any issues. The key clue came from the logs: the service started successfully but didn\'t mention metrics. This points to the absence of a metrics exporter in the container.\n\nSince the logs don\'t show any errors related to metrics, the problem is likely that the user-service isn\'t set up to expose metrics. The solution would involve adding a metrics exporter (like Prometheus) to the container and ensuring the Prometheus scrape job is correctly configured to collect those metrics. Without the exporter, there\'s nothing for Prometheus to scrape, leading to the empty results.\n\nThe user has already submitted the answer explaining the root cause and the mitigation steps. However, the sy