n\'t mention any issues, which suggests the application itself might not be exporting metrics. Maybe the service doesn\'t have a metrics endpoint, or the Prometheus scrape job isn\'t configured correctly.\n\nThe user tried using get_metrics with the query "up{job=\\"user-service\\"}" and got an empty result. That means Prometheus isn\'t finding any targets for that job. The next step would be to check the Prometheus configuration to see if the scrape job for user-service is set up properly. But since the user doesn\'t have access to the Prometheus config, maybe they can check the service\'s deployment to see if it\'s exposing metrics.\n\nLooking at the deployment, the user-service might not have a metrics exporter like Prometheus. If the container doesn\'t include such an exporter, Prometheus won\'t collect any metrics. Alternatively, the exporter might be running but on a different port or path than what\'s specified in the scrape job.\n\nThe user also tried checking the pods and deployments, confirming the pod is running. The logs don\'t show any errors related to metrics, so the issue is likely on the configuration side. The solution would involve ensuring the user-service includes a metrics exporter and that the Prometheus scrape job is correctly configured to target the right endpoint.\n\nSince the user can\'t modify the Prometheus config directly, they might need to update the deployment to include the exporter. For example, adding a sidecar container for Prometheus or configuring the existing container to expose metrics on a specific port. Then, they\'d need to adjust the scrape job to point to that port and path.\n\nIn the end, the answer should explain that the user-service isn\'t exporting metrics, leading to the absence in Prometheus. The fix involves adding a metrics exporter and verifying the scrape job settings.\n</think>\n\nsubmit_tool\n{"ans": "The user-service is not exposing metrics. The logs confirm the service is running, but no metrics exporter 