That confirms that Prometheus isn\'t finding any metrics from the user-service. Then, when they checked the pod logs, everything looked normal, but there was no mention of metrics. \n\nThe next step is to determine why the metrics aren\'t being exposed. The possible reasons are either the service doesn\'t have a metrics exporter (like Prometheus) running inside the container, or the exporter is configured incorrectly (wrong port, path, or job name in Prometheus). Since the logs don\'t show any errors related to the exporter, it\'s more likely that the exporter isn\'t present at all.\n\nTo fix this, the user needs to ensure that the user-service container includes a metrics exporter. If the container image doesn\'t have one, they might need to modify the deployment to include an exporter or use a different image that includes it. Additionally, checking the Prometheus scrape configuration to make sure it\'s pointing to the correct endpoints is crucial. \n\nSince the user has already confirmed the service is running and the logs are clean, the next logical step is to address the metrics exporter configuration. They might need to update the deployment YAML to include the necessary exporter, or adjust the Prometheus job settings to correctly target the service\'s metrics endpoint. \n\nIn the end, the answer should point out that the user-service isn\'t exporting metrics and suggest adding a metrics exporter to the container or verifying the Prometheus scrape job configuration. That way, the issue is both identified and addressed.\n</think>\n\nsubmit_tool\n{"ans": "The user-service is not exposing metrics. The logs confirm the service is running, but no metrics exporter is detected. Metrics are not being scraped by Prometheus, likely due to missing exporter configuration in the container or incorrect Prometheus scrape job settings. To resolve, ensure the user-service includes a metrics exporter (e.g., Prometheus) and verify the scrape job configuration in Prometheus."}', 