s don\'t mention it, maybe the container doesn\'t have a metrics server running. For example, if the service is using a framework like Go or Java, it might require an additional exporter like Prometheus HTTP server. If the container image doesn\'t include this, then metrics won\'t be available.\n\nAnother possibility is that the Prometheus scrape job is misconfigured. The user might have a scrape job that\'s targeting the wrong port or path. For instance, if the exporter is on port 9090 but the scrape job is set to 8080, that would explain the missing metrics. But since the user checked the service\'s port and it\'s mapped to 9090, that might not be the issue here.\n\nLooking at the kubectl logs, the user-service started successfully, but there\'s no mention of metrics. This suggests that the application isn\'t set up to expose metrics. The solution would be to either add a metrics exporter to the container or adjust the Prometheus scrape job to target the correct endpoint. \n\nSince the user can\'t modify the Prometheus configuration directly (as they don\'t have access to the scrape job definitions), the best course of action is to ensure the user-service includes a metrics exporter. This could involve updating the Docker image to include the necessary components or adding a sidecar container for the exporter. \n\nIn the end, the root cause is the absence of a metrics exporter in the user-service container. The mitigation steps would involve integrating the exporter into the service or adjusting the scrape job configuration to correctly target the metrics endpoint.\n</think>\n\nsubmit_tool\n{"ans": "The user-service is not exposing metrics. The logs confirm the service is running, but no metrics exporter is detected. Metrics are not being scraped by Prometheus, likely due to missing exporter configuration in the container or incorrect Prometheus scrape job settings. To resolve, ensure the user-service includes a metrics exporter (e.g., Prometheus) and verify the s